&lt;h1&gt;


Automating Weekly Content Publishing with a Node Script: Fixing CI Breakage and Adding Multi‑Platform Output
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I extended my content‑automation pipeline to generate Markdown, JSON and HTML for Medium, Substack and Bluesky in a single run, and I hardened the CI step that kept failing because of stray line‑breaks. The result is a reproducible, version‑controlled weekly post that lands on three platforms automatically.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My weekly “what‑I‑did‑this‑week” note lives in &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/10/content-automation/medium_en.md&lt;/code&gt;. The script that builds the post (&lt;code&gt;scripts/generate‑weekly.ts&lt;/code&gt;) writes the file, commits it, and triggers the CI pipeline that publishes to Medium, Substack and Bluesky.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two issues kept surfacing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CI failures&lt;/strong&gt; – The CI job would abort with &lt;code&gt;error: line 1: unexpected EOF while looking for matching \&lt;/code&gt;'&lt;code&gt;because the generated Markdown sometimes contained stray carriage returns (&lt;/code&gt;\r\n`) from Windows‑style line endings.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Missing platform payloads&lt;/strong&gt; – I only had a Markdown file for Medium/Substack. Bluesky required a JSON payload (&lt;code&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt;), but I was manually copying the content, which was error‑prone and not versioned.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The symptom was a broken pipeline every other week and a growing backlog of manual copy‑pastes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was to add a quick &lt;code&gt;sed&lt;/code&gt; command in the CI script to strip &lt;code&gt;\r&lt;/code&gt; characters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;bash&lt;br&gt;
sed -i 's/\r//g' content/**/*.md&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It worked for a couple of runs, but the underlying generator (&lt;code&gt;generate‑weekly.ts&lt;/code&gt;) still emitted Windows line endings on my local machine, so the issue resurfaced when I switched to a new laptop.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also tried to generate the Bluesky JSON by hand‑editing &lt;code&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt; each week. This worked once, but I introduced a typo (&lt;code&gt;"type": "avance"&lt;/code&gt; instead of &lt;code&gt;"type": "update"&lt;/code&gt;), which caused the Bluesky API to reject the payload with a &lt;code&gt;400 Bad Request&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both approaches were band‑aid solutions; I needed a deterministic, cross‑platform generation step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Normalize Line Endings at the Source
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a small utility function to &lt;code&gt;src/utils/normalize.ts&lt;/code&gt; and imported it into the generator. The function uses Node’s &lt;code&gt;os.EOL&lt;/code&gt; to enforce Unix line endings:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;ts&lt;br&gt;
// src/utils/normalize.ts&lt;br&gt;
export function normalizeEOL(content: string): string {&lt;br&gt;
// Replace Windows CRLF with LF&lt;br&gt;
return content.replace(/\r\n/g, '\n');&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, in &lt;code&gt;scripts/generate‑weekly.ts&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;`ts&lt;br&gt;
import { normalizeEOL } from '../src/utils/normalize';&lt;br&gt;
import fs from 'fs';&lt;br&gt;
import path from 'path';&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function writeFile(filePath: string, data: string) {&lt;br&gt;
const normalized = normalizeEOL(data);&lt;br&gt;
fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(filePath), { recursive: true });&lt;br&gt;
fs.writeFileSync(filePath, normalized, 'utf8');&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;// Example usage for Medium English post&lt;br&gt;
const mediumEn = &lt;code&gt;**Why I Let a Tiny Script Write My Weekly Posts (And How It Finally Stopped Screwing Up My CI)**\n\nWhen I started publishing...&lt;/code&gt;;&lt;br&gt;
writeFile('content/2026/10/10/content-automation/medium_en.md', mediumEn);&lt;br&gt;
`&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now every generated file uses LF line endings, eliminating the CI parsing error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Generate Platform‑Specific Payloads from a Single Source
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I introduced a JSON schema (&lt;code&gt;src/schemas/bluesky-payload.schema.json&lt;/code&gt;) and a generator that maps the Markdown front‑matter to the required JSON shape:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;`ts&lt;br&gt;
// src/generators/bluesky.ts&lt;br&gt;
import type { BlueskyPayload } from '../types';&lt;br&gt;
import fs from 'fs';&lt;br&gt;
import path from 'path';&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;export function generateBlueskyPayload(&lt;br&gt;
title: "string,"&lt;br&gt;
body: string,&lt;br&gt;
lang: 'en' | 'es'&lt;br&gt;
): BlueskyPayload {&lt;br&gt;
return {&lt;br&gt;
type: 'update',&lt;br&gt;
text: &lt;code&gt;${title}\n\n${body}&lt;/code&gt;,&lt;br&gt;
language: lang,&lt;br&gt;
};&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;// In generate‑weekly.ts&lt;br&gt;
import { generateBlueskyPayload } from '../src/generators/bluesky';&lt;br&gt;
import { writeFile } from './utils';&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const blueskyPayload = generateBlueskyPayload(&lt;br&gt;
'Weekly Review – Compliance Checklists &amp;amp; PDF Export',&lt;br&gt;
mediumEn,&lt;br&gt;
'en'&lt;br&gt;
);&lt;br&gt;
writeFile(&lt;br&gt;
'content/2026/10/10/appcheck-uhpcm/bluesky_es.json',&lt;br&gt;
JSON.stringify([blueskyPayload], null, 2)&lt;br&gt;
);&lt;br&gt;
`&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The resulting &lt;code&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt; (excerpt from the diff) now looks like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;json&lt;br&gt;
[&lt;br&gt;
{&lt;br&gt;
"type": "update",&lt;br&gt;
"text": "Weekly Review – Compliance Checklists &amp;amp; PDF Export\n\nWhen I started publishing a short “what‑I‑did‑this‑week” note on Medium, Substack...",&lt;br&gt;
"language": "en"&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
]&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice the correct &lt;code&gt;"type": "update"&lt;/code&gt; value and the consistent structure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Update Metadata Atomically
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; for each content bundle tracks which platforms have been generated. Previously I was toggling booleans manually, which caused mismatches (&lt;code&gt;"medium_generated": true&lt;/code&gt; while Substack remained &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;). I refactored the metadata update to be part of the same &lt;code&gt;writeFile&lt;/code&gt; flow:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;`ts&lt;br&gt;
// scripts/generate‑weekly.ts (continued)&lt;br&gt;
function updateMetadata(&lt;br&gt;
basePath: string,&lt;br&gt;
platforms: Partial&amp;gt;&lt;br&gt;
) {&lt;br&gt;
const metaPath = path.join(basePath, 'metadata.json');&lt;br&gt;
const current = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(metaPath, 'utf8'));&lt;br&gt;
const updated = { ...current, ...platforms };&lt;br&gt;
writeFile(metaPath, JSON.stringify(updated, null, 2));&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;// After generating all files&lt;br&gt;
updateMetadata('content/2026/10/10/appcheck-uhpcm', {&lt;br&gt;
medium_generated: true,&lt;br&gt;
substack_generated: true,&lt;br&gt;
bluesky_generated: true,&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;br&gt;
`&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diff now shows the updated &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;`diff&lt;br&gt;
@@ -17,10 +17,15 @@&lt;br&gt;
"pull_requests": 0,&lt;br&gt;
"releases": 0,&lt;br&gt;
"closed_issues": 0,&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt; "medium_generated": false,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; "substack_generated": false,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; "medium_generated": true,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; "substack_generated": true,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; "bluesky_generated": true,
"last_release": null
}
`&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Add a New Cron Endpoint (Bonus)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While touching the repo, I added a small cron route to send daily review reminders. The changelog entry (&lt;code&gt;content/.../changelog.md&lt;/code&gt;) references the new file &lt;code&gt;src/app/api/cron/review-reminders/route.ts&lt;/code&gt;. Here’s the core of that endpoint:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;`ts&lt;br&gt;
// src/app/api/cron/review-reminders/route.ts&lt;br&gt;
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';&lt;br&gt;
import { sendReminderEmails } from '../../../services/email';&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;export async function GET() {&lt;br&gt;
try {&lt;br&gt;
await sendReminderEmails();&lt;br&gt;
return NextResponse.json({ status: 'ok' });&lt;br&gt;
} catch (err) {&lt;br&gt;
console.error('Cron error:', err);&lt;br&gt;
return NextResponse.json({ status: 'error', error: err.message }, { status: 500 });&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;br&gt;
`&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It runs on Vercel’s cron schedule (&lt;code&gt;0 9 * * *&lt;/code&gt;) and ensures the checklist&lt;/p&gt;




