&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;ReceiptDesk AI 🧾🛡️&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/satanic47/receiptdesk-ai/LICENSE" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/58dd4da42df86c1864adaeef729174bec2cc4fe94d46a73afd38424fb2da9340/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f4c6963656e73652d4d49542d656d6572616c642e737667" alt="License: MIT"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="https://python.org" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/c1066d686f94641187c8281f17ab8cb61ddffcf350f553443643b96f5cc1f927/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f507974686f6e2d332e31302b2d626c75652e737667" alt="Python: 3.10+"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="https://fastapi.tiangolo.com" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/9500629826573c187231962dcc62cb056c5854144158edcffa929be0fbd095ec/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f466173744150492d302e3131352b2d7465616c2e737667" alt="FastAPI"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemma" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/808daea841bd49e273dbdc901afc73a98cf9f16d846b5b173064b9b7ed22cb07/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f4f70656e2d2d536f7572636525323041492d476f6f676c6525323047656d6d612d6f72616e67652e737667" alt="Open-Source AI: Gemma 2"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="https://tigerdata.cloud" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/0e81be58607217469398a3d3de73288a6565923146c221af4f9d70d899230f49/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f566563746f7225323044422d5469676572253230446174612532307067766563746f722d707572706c652e737667" alt="Vector DB: Tiger Data pgvector"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/satanic47/receiptdesk-ai/render.yaml" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/d9b2b5643442f04b7a27dcc14df747ac130ae4af4a8ddc8e56ef32c5995b19b7/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f4465706c6f792d52656e6465722d3436453342372e737667" alt="Deploy on Render"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;100% Privacy-First Invoice &amp;amp; Receipt Auditor with Open-Source AI for Freelancers &amp;amp; Contractors.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Automatically parses receipts, classifies IRS Schedule C business deductions, detects duplicate billings, and searches historical records with vector embeddings — &lt;strong&gt;without your confidential client rates and banking data ever leaving your laptop.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Built for the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week2-2026-10-12" rel="nofollow"&gt;Hacktoberfest 2026 Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 2 (Side Hustle)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;💡 The Problem &amp;amp; Why Freelancers Pay For This&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Freelancers and solo contractors lose &lt;strong&gt;thousands of dollars every tax season&lt;/strong&gt; in missed business expense write-offs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Receipt Fatigue:&lt;/strong&gt; Manually sorting hundreds of PDF receipts, SaaS invoices, and rideshare emails across bank accounts takes hours of unbillable time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Cloud Privacy Dilemma:&lt;/strong&gt; Freelancers handling confidential client NDAs, proprietary software rates, and sensitive financial spreadsheets refuse to upload raw bank statements and client invoices to closed third-party cloud APIs (OpenAI, Anthropic).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audit Exposure:&lt;/strong&gt; Misclassifying dining (strictly 50% IRS limit) or expensing assets…&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/satanic47/receiptdesk-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;ReceiptDesk AI is open-source under the MIT License. The codebase is structured cleanly in Python and FastAPI:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/core/gemma_engine.py: Google Gemma open-weight extraction grammar and prompt reasoning pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/core/vector_store.py: Hybrid vector database supporting Tiger Data pgvector for cloud indexing and local SQLite vector storage for 100% offline isolation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/core/tax_rules.py: IRS Schedule C knowledge base with keyword vectors and deduction limitation rules (50% meal limit, $2,500 de minimis safe harbor).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/core/parser.py: Ingestion pipeline supporting PDF text extraction (pypdf), text streams, and duplicate collision guards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/core/audit_analyzer.py: Audit exposure risk scoring (0–100) and Schedule C CSV export generator.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app/static/: Interactive Tailwind CSS single-page dashboard with real-time Chart.js visualizer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;tests/: 14 comprehensive unit and integration tests covering parser idempotency, deduction math, and vector similarity (100% passing).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How I Built It&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ReceiptDesk AI is designed around an open-source AI stack from the ground up:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Open-Weight AI Core: Google Gemma 2&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For extracting structured data from messy invoice text, ReceiptDesk AI uses Google's open-weight Gemma 2 (gemma-2-2b-it / gemma-2-9b-it).&lt;br&gt;
Unlike giant closed APIs that require sending full documents over the internet, Gemma runs locally via Ollama or lightweight on-device inference:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It parses line items, tax lines, and payment dates into strict JSON schemas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It detects handwritten notes or irregular merchant descriptions and standardizes them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the local inference server is running on a low-resource laptop, ReceiptDesk AI includes a deterministic tokenizer fallback that uses Gemma's extraction grammar without dropping precision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Semantic Vector Memory: Tiger Data pgvector&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Freelancers often wonder: "What did I categorize this obscure SaaS tool under three months ago?" or "Did my client already reimburse this expense?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this, ReceiptDesk AI uses Tiger Data pgvector (best-use-of-tiger-data):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a receipt is processed, an embedding vector is generated and stored in a PostgreSQL vector(128) table.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you search for "cloud database" or "client lunch", pgvector performs cosine distance similarity (1 - (embedding &amp;lt;=&amp;gt; query)), instantly surfacing past invoices, duplicate charges, and suggested Schedule C categories.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A local SQLite fallback ensures that if you are completely offline on a flight without Wi-Fi, the vector search continues running locally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IRS Schedule C Rules Engine&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core tax logic encodes IRS guidelines:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meals &amp;amp; Entertainment: Automatically caps write-offs at 50% per IRS Section 274(n).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;De Minimis Safe Harbor: Flags single equipment purchases exceeding $2,500 (under IRS Notice 2015-82) that cannot be immediately expensed and must be depreciated over multiple years.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Telecom &amp;amp; Utilities: Calculates reasonable 80% home-office business allocations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cloud Deployment &amp;amp; Custom .tech Domain (shivysidinfo.tech)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make the application instantly accessible to freelancers worldwide:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Custom .tech Domain (shivysidinfo.tech): Claimed directly through the Hacktoberfest 2026 partner promotion via get.tech / Namify. The domain serves as the official production gateway for both the interactive ReceiptDesk AI auditor and my Halloween 2026 journey tracker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Render Cloud Service: Fully containerized with a production Dockerfile and declarative render.yaml Blueprint. Render handles automatic Let's Encrypt SSL certificates, HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection, and continuous deployment from GitHub origin/main.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why Does Open Innovation Matter?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open-source AI isn't just an alternative to closed models — for financial applications, open innovation is the only architecture that actually makes sense:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zero Compromise on Client Financial Privacy: Freelancers frequently handle confidential client project briefs, NDA-protected vendor names, and sensitive billing details. With an open-weight model running locally on your laptop, zero bytes of financial data leave your device. No OpenAI servers, no Anthropic data retention policies, no risk of client data leaking into next quarter's training set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zero Marginal Per-Token Cost: A freelancer sorting 500 historical receipts shouldn't have to worry about metering, rate limits, or per-token API charges. Running Gemma locally costs $0.00.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No Vendor Lock-In or Deprecation: Closed APIs change pricing, adjust moderation filters, and deprecate models without warning. With open weights, the software you rely on today will work exactly the same way five years from now on your laptop with no internet connection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My Agent Session&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This entire project — from the IRS tax categorization rules and Tiger Data pgvector integration to the FastAPI backend, Chart.js visualizer, and 14-test suite — was pair-programmed with Google Antigravity using DeepMind's advanced coding agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent helped:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architect the hybrid vector store supporting both Tiger Data pgvector and local offline SQLite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write unit tests in pytest verifying 100% test pass rate in under 1 second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Containerize the application with Dockerfile and render.yaml for 1-click cloud deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wire custom DNS and SSL routing for shivysidinfo.tech.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My First User&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To test whether someone would actually pay for ReceiptDesk AI, I handed it to my friend Sarah, a freelance brand identity designer who was dreading her quarterly estimated tax filing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her Problem: She had 34 PDF invoices and coffee shop receipts from Q3 sitting in her "Downloads" folder, unorganized and unsorted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Test: She visited &lt;a href="https://shivysidinfo.tech" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://shivysidinfo.tech&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
, clicked "Scan or Upload", dragged all 34 files into ReceiptDesk AI, and watched the dashboard process them in under 20 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Result:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ReceiptDesk AI identified $412.50 in deductible business expenses she had forgotten about (including a Figma seat upgrade, font licensing from Type Network, and a client strategy lunch).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It caught a duplicate charge where Adobe had billed her twice in the same billing cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her Reaction:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Wait, so this runs completely on my MacBook without sending my bank details to ChatGPT? I would happily pay $15 a month just for the duplicate detection and TurboTax export alone."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prize Categories&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am officially entering ReceiptDesk AI in the following partner categories:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Best Use of Gemma (best-use-of-gemma): Uses Google's open-weight Gemma 2 model to extract line items, standardize invoice text, and reason over tax categories locally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Best Use of Tiger Data (best-use-of-tiger-data): Integrates Tiger Data PostgreSQL with pgvector to store expense embeddings and execute semantic similarity queries across historical receipts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Best Use of Render (best-use-of-render): Fully containerized with render.yaml and Dockerfile for seamless automated deployment on Render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Best Use of a .tech Domain (best-use-of-a-tech-domain): Deployed live on custom domain &lt;a href="https://shivysidinfo.tech" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://shivysidinfo.tech&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
claimed through the Hacktoberfest get.tech partner promotion. Features automated Let's Encrypt SSL certificates, high-performance edge delivery, and DNS routing for both the live ReceiptDesk AI invoice auditor and Halloween 2026 challenge activities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built with ❤️ by Sudhir Sharma (&lt;a class="mentioned-user" href="https://dev.to/satanic47"&gt;@satanic47&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
) for freelancers and open-source AI enthusiasts everywhere. Live at shivysidinfo.tech — feedback and PRs welcome on GitHub!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Adding Per‑Container CPU &amp;amp; Memory Limits to Docker‑Compose for pgvector
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I added &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cpus&lt;/code&gt; to the PostgreSQL service in &lt;code&gt;docker‑compose.yml&lt;/code&gt; to prevent the dev database from starving other containers. The change caps the container at 512 MiB RAM and 1 CPU, stabilizing the local environment without affecting production.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the &lt;strong&gt;bienestar‑integral‑kb&lt;/strong&gt; stack locally on my MacBook Pro (M1, 16 GB RAM) started to feel flaky after I added a couple of AI‑powered micro‑services. The PostgreSQL container (&lt;code&gt;pgvector/pgvector:pg16&lt;/code&gt;) would occasionally consume all available memory, causing the Docker daemon to kill other containers (e.g., the FastAPI inference service). Docker Desktop shows the following symptom:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[2026-10-10T14:32:12.345Z] container is out of memory: kill process 12 (postgres) score 999 or sacrifice child
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The error manifested as intermittent &lt;code&gt;502 Bad Gateway&lt;/code&gt; responses from the API gateway because the dependent services were being OOM‑killed. The root cause was that Docker Compose, by default, gives each container unrestricted access to host resources. In a multi‑service dev environment that’s a recipe for “noisy neighbor” problems.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to use Docker Desktop’s &lt;strong&gt;Resources → Advanced&lt;/strong&gt; UI to limit the whole Docker engine to, say, 8 GB RAM. While that prevented total exhaustion, it also throttled services that legitimately needed more memory (e.g., the embedding model). I needed a &lt;strong&gt;per‑container&lt;/strong&gt; limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I then tried the &lt;code&gt;deploy.resources.limits&lt;/code&gt; block that Docker Compose supports:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pgvector/pgvector:pg16&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;deploy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;resources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;limits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;cpus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;1"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;memory&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;512M&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, the &lt;code&gt;deploy&lt;/code&gt; key is ignored unless the Compose file is run in &lt;strong&gt;Swarm mode&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;docker stack deploy&lt;/code&gt;). Running &lt;code&gt;docker compose up&lt;/code&gt; (the v2 CLI) silently ignored those limits, leaving the problem unchanged.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next approach was to switch the Compose file version to &lt;code&gt;3.8&lt;/code&gt; and use the &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cpus&lt;/code&gt; top‑level keys, which are supported in version 2.x files. After a quick read of the docs, I realized that &lt;strong&gt;Compose file version 2.4&lt;/strong&gt; (or later) allows these fields without Swarm. That’s the path I took.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Update the Compose version
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The original &lt;code&gt;docker-compose.yml&lt;/code&gt; started with version &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pgvector/pgvector:pg16&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;container_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ismerely_postgres&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;restart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;unless-stopped&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_USER&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I bumped it to &lt;code&gt;2.4&lt;/code&gt; to guarantee support for resource constraints:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2.4"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


2. Add &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cpus&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diff from commit &lt;code&gt;00f0bbc2&lt;/code&gt; shows the exact lines added:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@&lt;/span&gt; services:
image: pgvector/pgvector:pg16
container_name: ismerely_postgres
restart: unless-stopped
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  mem_limit: 512m
+  cpus: "1"
&lt;/span&gt;   environment:
POSTGRES_USER: ...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Full updated service definition:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pgvector/pgvector:pg16&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;container_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ismerely_postgres&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;restart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;unless-stopped&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;mem_limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;512m&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# cap RAM at 512 MiB&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;cpus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;1"&lt;/span&gt;                &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# limit to a single logical CPU&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_USER&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;${POSTGRES_USER}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_PASSWORD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;${POSTGRES_DB}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;volumes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ports&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;5432:5432"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


3. Verify the limits
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After &lt;code&gt;docker compose up -d&lt;/code&gt;, I inspected the container:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;docker inspect ismerely_postgres &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{{json .HostConfig.Memory}}'&lt;/span&gt; | jq &lt;span class="nb"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
536870912   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# 512 MiB in bytes&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;docker inspect ismerely_postgres &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{{json .HostConfig.NanoCpus}}'&lt;/span&gt; | jq &lt;span class="nb"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
1000000000   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# 1 CPU (1e9 nanocpus)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Running &lt;code&gt;docker stats ismerely_postgres&lt;/code&gt; confirmed the caps:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;CONTAINER ID   NAME                CPU %     MEM USAGE / LIMIT
c1d2e3f4g5h6    ismerely_postgres   0.12%     210MiB / 512MiB
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The PostgreSQL container now stays comfortably under its limit, and the other services no longer get OOM‑killed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Why &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; vs &lt;code&gt;mem_reservation&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I considered using &lt;code&gt;mem_reservation&lt;/code&gt; (soft limit) but opted for a hard cap (&lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt;) because the dev environment is already memory‑constrained. A soft limit would still allow the DB to balloon under load, re‑triggering the original issue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Compatibility notes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Docker Engine ≥ 1.13&lt;/strong&gt; is required for &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cpus&lt;/code&gt; in Compose v2.4.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;These keys are ignored by Docker Swarm (&lt;code&gt;docker stack deploy&lt;/code&gt;). If the project ever migrates to Swarm or Kubernetes, you’ll need to translate the constraints to the respective platform’s syntax (&lt;code&gt;deploy.resources.limits&lt;/code&gt; for Swarm, &lt;code&gt;resources.limits&lt;/code&gt; in a K8s pod spec).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Per‑container resource constraints in Docker Compose are a lightweight, production‑agnostic way to prevent a single service from monopolizing host resources during local development.&lt;/strong&gt; Using &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cpus&lt;/code&gt; (available from Compose file version 2.4) gives you deterministic behavior without the overhead of Swarm or Kubernetes.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What's Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Add healthchecks&lt;/strong&gt; to the PostgreSQL service so Docker can automatically restart it if it becomes unresponsive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Instrument container metrics&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;code&gt;cAdvisor&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;Prometheus&lt;/code&gt; to monitor actual usage trends over time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explore dynamic limits&lt;/strong&gt;: write a small script that adjusts &lt;code&gt;mem_limit&lt;/code&gt; based on the size of the dataset loaded into pgvector during CI runs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Roberto Luna Osorio – Full Stack Developer &amp;amp; Project Lead&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Playa del Carmen, México  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


vibecoding #buildinpublic #docker #docker-compose #postgresql #pgvector #devops #resource-limits
&lt;/h1&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building Building Ismerely KB from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/bienestar-integral-kb&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-11&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/over-mocking-is-a-governance-problem/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tddbuddy.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Related reading: &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/agents-pattern-match-your-smells/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Agents Pattern-Match Your Test Smells&lt;/a&gt; named over-mocking as one of five smells; this post is the dedicated argument backed by the empirical study. &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/the-contract-test-is-the-only-witness/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Contract Test Is the Only Witness the Agent Cannot Author&lt;/a&gt; owns the seams the allowlist opens. &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/instruction-files-are-not-the-discipline/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Instruction File Is Not the Discipline&lt;/a&gt; is the same failure mode at a smaller scale.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study counted the mocks. The answer was a policy question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A 2026 paper by Hora and Robbes (arxiv 2602.00409, MSR 2026) measured 1.2 million commits across public repositories, isolated the 48,563 authored by coding agents, and compared the rate at which those commits introduce test doubles against the rate at which humans introduce them. Agent test commits add mocks 36% of the time. Non-agent test commits add them 26% of the time. The agents concentrate on a single test-double style where humans spread across stubs, fakes, spies, and mocks. The suites that result are more brittle than the human baseline on the measures the paper reports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The gap is directional, not deterministic. Ten percentage points on a measured dataset is a clear signal; it is not evidence that agents mock everything in sight. What the signal describes is a tilt. A codebase that already leans on mocks gets leaned on harder. A codebase that leans harder teaches the next session to lean harder still. The industry's instinct is to catch this in review. The industry's instinct will not scale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over-mocking stopped being a review smell on the day the sampler got faster than the reviewer. What has to replace the review is governance, and the convention layer is where the fix lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Numbers Land on a Governance Question
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The MSR study is not a thought piece. It is 1.2 million commits, 48,563 of them agent-authored, measured on a comparison the review-pass-at-a-time workflow has no way to see. The 36% versus 26% delta is the headline. The subsidiary findings matter as much. Agent-generated test commits cluster narrowly on one test-double style, where human commits distribute across the toolbox in a way that tracks the shape of the problem. Brittleness on the agent-generated portion reads higher than the human baseline on the paper's measures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That shape is a governance shape, not a taste shape. A review-taste finding would show scattered individual lapses the reviewer could flag one at a time. A governance finding shows a measurable, directional, dataset-scale tilt that recurs regardless of which reviewer is on the PR. The reviewer did not cause the tilt. The reviewer cannot fix the tilt. The tilt is a property of how the sampler reads the codebase and extends it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The intervention that moves the number lives at the layer that shapes what the sampler sees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Governance beats hunting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Review-Smell Framing Catches One at Reading Speed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The earlier post in this arc listed over-mocking as one of five smells agents reproduce from the local suite. That framing is honest and it is necessary. It is also insufficient on its own. The review-smell lens looks at a single PR, surfaces a single mock, asks a question, and moves on. The workflow fits the production rate of a human writing one test at a time. It does not fit the production rate of a sampler writing twenty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The math is against the reviewer structurally. A reviewer who catches eight mocks in a diff of twenty tests has caught eight mocks and shipped twelve. The twelve ship with precedent weight: each becomes an example the next session samples. Next week's PR arrives with thirty tests built on top of the twelve. The reviewer who was running an uphill fight starts running one on a steeper hill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the review-smell framing earns is the diagnosis. What it does not earn is the fix. The fix is one level up, at the convention the reviewer is defending. A reviewer defending a strong convention catches the exceptions. A reviewer defending nothing in particular is reading diffs at reading speed against a sampler running at generation speed, and the arithmetic has one answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The smell outruns the reviewer. The reviewer's job is to hold the convention that decided whether the mock was allowed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Precedent Is Load-Bearing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agents mock what the codebase mocks. Every merged PR adds a vote for the pattern it carried, and the next session reads the vote. A suite with a mock-heavy convention will produce mock-heavy tests at generation throughput for as long as the convention stands. The precedent is not a historical artifact. It is the active specification for what the next hundred tests will look like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the mechanism the earlier posts in this arc have been circling. Agents are faithful pattern-matchers. The faithfulness is a feature when the pattern is good and a liability when the pattern is a smell. A codebase that teaches "every collaborator is a mock" by example gets that teaching reproduced with structural fidelity, in every file, at generation speed. The sampler does not know the convention is a smell. The sampler knows the convention is what the suite says to do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The suite is self-instructing. The question is which instruction it carries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


No Mocks Without an Allowlist Entry
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flipping the default is the move. Mocks are not banned. Mocks are not allowed without a named seam. A static analyzer rejects &lt;code&gt;Mock&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; everywhere by default and permits it only in paths the team has listed: the specific boundary files that touch external systems the suite cannot legitimately stand up in process. The rule runs in CI. A test that adds a mock outside the allowlist fails the gate. The gate does not negotiate at the PR layer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The composition primitive the suite reaches for by default is not a mock. It is a builder plus a real domain object:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Loyalty_members_get_a_ten_percent_discount_on_orders_over_fifty_dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;aCartReadyForCheckout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forCustomer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;aLoyaltyMember&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;containing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;aBookCosting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receipt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;receipt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;discount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Should&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Be&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;receipt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Should&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Be&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;54&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The mock-heavy version of the same test would set up three mocks, stub the pricing service, stub the membership service, stub the repository, call the method under test, and verify an invocation on one of the mocks. The mock version tests the mock. The builder version tests the behavior. The second one is shorter, reads in the domain's vocabulary, and does not grant the next session a precedent that says "wire these three mocks together."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The honest concession has one shape. Some seams need mocks. A payment gateway cannot run inside a unit suite. A third-party API with rate limits cannot be exercised on every PR. A filesystem call to a device that is not there has to be faked. The allowlist names those seams by path. The rule is not "no mocks." The rule is "no mocks without an allowlist entry," and the entry is the convention the team signs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An allowlist with ten names is a convention. A codebase with no allowlist is also a convention, and it is the one the study measured.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Contracts Own the Seams the Allowlist Opens
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the seam where mocks are legitimate, the mock is not the whole story. The mock verifies the local shape of the call. The contract test verifies the truth at the seam. The companion post on contract tests pinned the mechanism: a test the agent wrote against code the same agent wrote is a mirror, and the integration seam is where a second author lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pair the allowlist with the pact. Every allowlisted mock has an attached contract test, authored on the counterparty's cadence, stored outside the writable tree of the sampler that owns the client. The mock keeps the unit suite fast. The contract keeps the mock honest. Remove one and the other is defeatable. Keep both and the seam is covered by two independent witnesses, which is what the seam has needed since before the word "agent" meant what it means now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mock verifies the shape the client sent. The contract verifies the shape the counterparty will accept. The two are not substitutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Governance Is a Test-Design Property, Not a Policy Document
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A markdown file in the root of the repository that says "avoid mocks" is theatre. The writer-agent can read it, agree with it, and ignore it on the next sample. An unstructured directive that lives in prose and depends on the sampler to self-police is a request, not a rule. The request is honored exactly as reliably as the sampler finds it convenient to honor, which is unreliably. The study measured the unreliability directly, in points of a tilt, across a dataset larger than any team's workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The nuance matters. The argument is not that an instruction file can never work. The argument is that an unstructured directive the writer-agent can see and reinterpret is theatre; a structural rule the writer-agent cannot bypass is design. A lint rule is design. A codeowners entry on the test directory is design. A failing CI gate is design. An AGENTS.md paragraph that no gate enforces is prose. Prose captures intent. Structure enforces it. The enforcement stack has been the house frame throughout this series: prose, tests, contracts, policies, governed judgment, production as verdict. The policy file sits at the prose tier. The convention lives at the policy tier. Confusing the two is how teams install discipline that does not install.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost of installing the convention is small. Ten lines of analyzer configuration, one week to migrate the current offenders, one honest conversation per named seam. The payoff compounds across every future test the sampler ever writes against the codebase. The asymmetry is the argument.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study counted the mocks. The answer was never going to come from the PR queue. The answer lives at the convention layer, where a rule the sampler cannot talk its way around decides whether the next thousand tests look like the ones the paper measured or like the ones the team meant to ship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over-mocking is a governance problem. Governance is where the governance work goes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quick answer:&lt;/strong&gt; browsers hide non-standard response headers from cross-origin scripts. Send &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Expose-Headers: PAYMENT-REQUIRED, PAYMENT-RESPONSE&lt;/code&gt; on your 402 and paid responses, and answer the &lt;code&gt;OPTIONS&lt;/code&gt; preflight with &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Headers&lt;/code&gt; that includes &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-SIGNATURE&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the spec puts in headers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the x402 v2 HTTP transport, the server sends the payment terms base64-encoded in &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-REQUIRED&lt;/code&gt; with the 402. The client retries with &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-SIGNATURE&lt;/code&gt;, and the server confirms with &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-RESPONSE&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-REQUIRED&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-RESPONSE&lt;/code&gt; are response headers that aren’t CORS-safelisted, so a script on another origin sees the 402 status but gets &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; for them unless you list them in &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Expose-Headers&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-SIGNATURE&lt;/code&gt; is a request header: it goes in &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Headers&lt;/code&gt;, not the expose list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Minimal Worker headers
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CORS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;PAYMENT-SIGNATURE, X-PAYMENT, Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Access-Control-Expose-Headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;PAYMENT-REQUIRED, PAYMENT-RESPONSE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// OPTIONS -&amp;gt; new Response(null, { status: 204, headers: CORS })&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// add CORS to the 402 and to the paid 200&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Test it with curl
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# the 402 exposes the terms&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-D&lt;/span&gt; - &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; /dev/null &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'^access-control-expose-headers'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# the preflight allows the payment header&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-D&lt;/span&gt; - &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; /dev/null &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; OPTIONS &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Origin: https://example.com'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Access-Control-Request-Method: GET'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Access-Control-Request-Headers: payment-signature'&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'^access-control-allow-headers'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or paste the URL into the free &lt;a href="https://x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev/tools/x402-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;x402 endpoint checker&lt;/a&gt;, which warns when the 402 has no CORS headers or doesn’t expose &lt;code&gt;PAYMENT-REQUIRED&lt;/code&gt;. It doesn’t send a preflight, so run the second curl command for that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which headers does x402 v2 use over HTTP?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PAYMENT-REQUIRED (server to client, with the 402), PAYMENT-SIGNATURE (client to server) and PAYMENT-RESPONSE (server to client, with the settlement result). All three carry base64-encoded JSON.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What about X-PAYMENT?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;X-PAYMENT is the older v1 request header. Some servers accept it alongside PAYMENT-SIGNATURE for older clients, so allow both in Access-Control-Allow-Headers if you support both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do server-side agents need CORS?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. CORS only applies to scripts running in a browser. Server-side clients and agents read every header regardless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sources: the &lt;a href="https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/transports-v2/http.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;x402 v2 HTTP transport spec&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/tools/payments/x402/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloudflare Agents x402 docs&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Access-Control-Expose-Headers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN on Access-Control-Expose-Headers&lt;/a&gt;. More checks: &lt;a href="https://x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev/guides/test-x402-endpoint-curl" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to test an x402 endpoint with curl&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev/guides/x402-payment-required-header-cors" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev&lt;/a&gt;. Free tool: the &lt;a href="https://x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev/tools/x402-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;x402 endpoint checker&lt;/a&gt;. If you'd rather not build it yourself, the &lt;a href="https://x402-seller-kit.soloearn.workers.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;x402 Seller Kit&lt;/a&gt; ($29) is a tested Cloudflare Worker template.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a Security Center system slows down, the first suspect is usually the Archiver and the second is the network. In my experience the database is the cause more often than either, and it is the one nobody checked, because SQL Server was installed by the Genetec installer five years ago and has not been looked at since.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security Center is a database-backed application. The Directory role keeps its entire configuration, every event, every alarm, every audit entry, and every user session in SQL Server. Each Archiver keeps a database of its own that indexes every video file it writes. The Access Manager, the LPR Manager, and the Health Monitor each have one too. When those databases are healthy the system feels instant. When they are not, the symptoms show up everywhere except the place they started: Security Desk takes twenty seconds to log in, the timeline crawls, alarms arrive late, and the Directory restarts itself at 03:00 for no reason anyone can find.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the database side of the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/configuring-and-tuning-genetec-security-center/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tuning guide&lt;/a&gt;, and it is the section of that work that most often turns a slow system into a fast one without buying anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scope.&lt;/strong&gt; This covers SQL Server as Genetec uses it, on Windows, in the on-premises deployment pattern that still accounts for most enterprise systems. The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/genetec-security-center-architecture-roles-workstations/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;architecture article&lt;/a&gt; covers where each role and its database should sit. This one covers what to do with the databases once they are there.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Express or Standard: the decision most systems never made
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Security Center installer will happily deploy SQL Server Express, and a large share of production systems are still running on it because nobody revisited the choice once the system went live. Express is free and it is fine for small systems. It also has hard limits that do not announce themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Limit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;SQL Server Express&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it means under Security Center&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database size&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10 GB per database&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The Directory database on a busy multi-site system reaches this within a few years of event history. When it does, writes fail and the Directory stops.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Roughly 1.4 GB buffer pool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Frequently accessed data no longer fits in cache. Every timeline query goes to disk.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Compute&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lesser of one socket or four cores&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Parallel query plans are unavailable. Reports and audit searches serialize.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SQL Server Agent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Not included&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No built-in scheduler for maintenance. Backups and index work have to be scripted through Task Scheduler.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 10 GB limit is the one that causes outages. It is enforced per database, so the Directory database is the one that hits it, and it hits it silently. The first sign is usually an event that fails to write, followed by a Directory role that goes unhealthy, followed by a phone call. If the Directory database is over 6 GB today and growing, plan the move to Standard now rather than during the incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My rule: Express is acceptable for a single-site system under roughly 150 cameras with short event retention and a documented plan for what happens at 8 GB. Anything federated, anything with access control at scale, anything with LPR, and anything where the Directory is business-critical belongs on Standard. The licence cost is small against the cost of a Directory outage on a site that depends on it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Max server memory
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SQL Server takes as much memory as the operating system will give it. On a dedicated database server that is the correct behaviour. On a Security Center server where SQL shares the box with the Directory service, the Genetec Server service, and whatever else got installed, it starves everything else and the Directory starts paging. This is the single most common configuration gap I find, and it is a one-line fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set max server memory explicitly. The number depends on total RAM and what else runs on the host. A defensible starting point on a server that runs the Directory role alongside SQL:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Total RAM   Reserve for OS + Genetec   SQL max server memory&lt;br&gt;
16 GB       8 GB                       8192 MB&lt;br&gt;
32 GB       12 GB                      20480 MB&lt;br&gt;
64 GB       16 GB                      49152 MB&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;EXEC sys.sp_configure N’show advanced options’, N’1’;&lt;br&gt;
RECONFIGURE;&lt;br&gt;
EXEC sys.sp_configure N’max server memory (MB)’, N’20480’;&lt;br&gt;
RECONFIGURE;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then watch it. If the buffer cache hit ratio stays high and Page Life Expectancy is stable, the cap is fine. If SQL is consistently at its cap and the Directory is healthy, raise it. If the Directory is paging, lower it. The goal is a cap that keeps the working set in memory without starving the application that owns the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set min server memory as well, to roughly half the max, so that a memory-hungry process cannot squeeze SQL down to nothing during a backup or a video export.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Recovery model and backups
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every Security Center database should be in the Simple recovery model unless you have a specific reason to run Full, and that reason is usually log shipping or point-in-time restore, neither of which most security systems use. Full recovery without regular log backups produces a transaction log that grows until the disk fills, at which point the database stops accepting writes and the Directory stops with it. I have watched a 200 GB transaction log take down a Directory whose actual data was 4 GB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ALTER DATABASE [Directory] SET RECOVERY SIMPLE;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check every database, not just the Directory. Archiver databases are created with whatever the model default was at the time, and the model default is not always what you expect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For backups, use the Server Admin backup schedule that Genetec provides for the Directory and role databases. It handles the Genetec side correctly, it is aware of the roles, and it produces backups that Genetec support will accept. Back up to a different volume than the database files and copy the result off the host. A backup on the same disk as the database it protects is not a backup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Test the restore.&lt;/strong&gt; Once a year at least, restore the Directory backup onto a lab server and bring a Directory up on it. A backup that has never been restored is an assumption, and Security Center configuration is exactly the kind of thing that turns out to have been silently incomplete when it is finally needed.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Autogrowth, in megabytes, not percent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SQL Server grows a data file when it fills. The default growth increment on older installs is 1 MB for data and 10 percent for the log. Growing 1 MB at a time on an event-heavy Directory database means thousands of growth operations, each one briefly blocking writes and fragmenting the file on disk. Ten percent growth on a large log produces increasingly enormous growth events, each one taking longer and blocking longer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set fixed growth in megabytes on every Security Center database. For the Directory, 256 MB data and 128 MB log is a reasonable start on a system of any size. For Archiver databases, which index video rather than store events, 128 MB is usually enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ALTER DATABASE [Directory]&lt;br&gt;
MODIFY FILE (NAME = N’Directory’, FILEGROWTH = 256MB);&lt;br&gt;
ALTER DATABASE [Directory]&lt;br&gt;
MODIFY FILE (NAME = N’Directory_log’, FILEGROWTH = 128MB);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pre-size the files while you are there. If the Directory database is 5 GB and growing a gigabyte a year, size the data file to 8 GB now and let it grow in 256 MB steps from there. Growth events during operation are the thing you are trying to avoid, and pre-sizing avoids most of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enable instant file initialization by granting the SQL Server service account the Perform Volume Maintenance Tasks right. Data file growth then completes without zeroing the new space first. It does not apply to log files, which is one more reason to size the log sensibly up front.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


TempDB
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Directory uses TempDB heavily for report generation, audit searches, and the sort operations behind the timeline. On the default install TempDB is a single file on the system drive, which means it competes with the operating system and the Directory database for the same disk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Move TempDB to its own volume. Give it one data file per core up to eight, all the same size, and set them to grow in fixed megabyte increments. On a four-core Directory server, four 512 MB files and a 256 MB log is a sensible baseline. This is standard SQL Server practice rather than anything Genetec-specific, and it makes a measurable difference to report and search latency on busy systems.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Index maintenance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security Center writes events constantly and deletes them on a retention schedule. That pattern fragments indexes faster than most workloads, and a heavily fragmented Directory index turns a timeline query that should take milliseconds into one that takes seconds. The symptom operators report is “the system is slow in the afternoon,” because by afternoon the day’s events have arrived and the indexes are in their worst state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On SQL Server Standard, schedule a weekly job through SQL Server Agent that reorganizes indexes over 10 percent fragmented and rebuilds those over 30 percent, then updates statistics. On Express there is no Agent, so the same script runs from Windows Task Scheduler through &lt;code&gt;sqlcmd&lt;/code&gt;. Either way, run it in the quietest window the site has, which for a security system is usually not overnight, when the Archiver is at full load, but mid-morning on a weekday when recording is steady and nobody is running reports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sqlcmd -S .\SQLEXPRESS -E -Q “EXEC sp_MSforeachdb ‘USE [?]; IF DB_ID() &amp;gt; 4 BEGIN EXEC sp_updatestats; END‘“&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the minimum. A proper maintenance script that inspects fragmentation per index and acts accordingly is better, and the Ola Hallengren maintenance solution is the one I deploy on Standard installs because it does exactly that and it is free.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Event retention is a database setting wearing a Genetec costume
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Directory keeps every event, every alarm, and every audit trail entry until the retention period tells it to stop. The defaults are generous and on a busy access control site the event tables become the largest thing in the database within months. This is the growth that takes Express installs to their 10 GB limit and Standard installs to the point where index maintenance stops finishing in its window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set retention deliberately, per event type, in Server Admin. Decide how long you actually need access events, alarm history, and audit trails, and set those numbers. Ninety days of access events with a year of audit trail is a defensible baseline for a corporate site; a regulated facility will have a policy that sets the number for you. What is not defensible is leaving the default in place because nobody made the decision, and then discovering the decision was made for you by the disk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Archiver’s database is a different case. It indexes video files rather than storing them, so its size tracks the number of files rather than their duration. A system with short recording segments and many cameras produces a large Archiver database. That is normal, but it should be on the same maintenance schedule as the Directory, and it should never be on SQL Express on a large Archiver.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The failures, by symptom
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What operators report&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What is usually wrong&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where to look first&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Security Desk takes 15 to 30 seconds to log in&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Directory index fragmentation, or SQL memory starved&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fragmentation on the Directory’s user and entity tables; max server memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Timeline is slow to populate in the afternoon&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Index fragmentation from the day’s event writes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weekly maintenance job, and whether it is actually completing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Directory restarts overnight&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Transaction log or data file filled the disk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recovery model; free space on the SQL volume&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Events stop appearing, Directory role unhealthy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Express 10 GB limit reached&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Directory database size; event retention&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Everything is slow after a reboot for an hour&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cold buffer cache on an undersized memory cap&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Max and min server memory; total RAM&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reports time out&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TempDB on the system drive, or Express core limit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TempDB placement; edition&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is what I check on every Security Center database during a &lt;a href="https://hans.study/genetec-health-check/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;health check&lt;/a&gt;, in the order that finds the worst problems fastest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Edition, and the size of every database against the Express limit if it applies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Max and min server memory set explicitly, and appropriate for what else runs on the host.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Recovery model on every database, and the size of every transaction log.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Autogrowth in fixed megabytes on every file. Pre-sized data files. Instant file initialization enabled.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  TempDB on its own volume with multiple equal files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  A maintenance job that exists, runs, and finishes. The job log, not the schedule, is the evidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Backups on a different volume, copied off the host, with a restore that has been tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Event retention set deliberately and documented, per event type.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Antivirus exclusions on the SQL data, log, and TempDB paths. The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/configuring-and-tuning-genetec-security-center/#antivirus" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tuning guide&lt;/a&gt; has the list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this needs new hardware. Most of it is an afternoon. On the systems where I have done it the difference in login time and timeline responsiveness is the kind of thing operators notice and mention, which for infrastructure work is the highest compliment available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You know that moment when you’re staring at your screen, and you just can’t seem to find the right way to express what you want in code? It’s frustrating, right? Well, I've been diving deep into the world of AI coding agents, and I can’t help but feel a mix of excitement and skepticism. Sure, these AI tools can churn out lines of code faster than I can finish my morning coffee, but I've noticed something peculiar: they generate a ton of code, but not necessarily more software. Ever wondered why that is?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Promise of AI Coding Agents
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I first started experimenting with AI coding agents, I was like a kid in a candy store. With tools like GitHub Copilot and OpenAI Codex offering suggestions and even generating entire functions, it felt like I’d stumbled upon a cheat code for coding. I remember my first successful interaction with Copilot—suggesting a function in Python to parse JSON data. It wasn't just about getting the code written; it was about the way the AI could fill in gaps in my knowledge and help me think through the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But as I continued to use these tools, I realized they weren’t a silver bullet. Sure, they could generate code snippets in seconds, but when it came to building comprehensive software applications? That’s where things got tricky. It’s almost like having a really knowledgeable friend who can recite facts at whim, but when it comes to constructing a coherent argument or narrative—they fall flat. This led me to my first “aha” moment: AI coding agents are fantastic at generating code, but they lack the holistic view of software development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Code vs. Software: What's the Difference?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let’s break it down. Generating lines of code is one thing, but creating software involves architecture, design, usability, and long-term maintenance. In my experience, I've found that the best software emerges from a blend of creativity, planning, and iterative feedback. AI can help automate repetitive tasks and cut down development time, but it can’t replace the nuance of human understanding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For instance, I once used Copilot to generate a REST API in Flask. The code was solid, but integrating it into my existing architecture was a mess. The AI didn’t know the specific context of my application, nor could it anticipate user interactions. I ended up spending just as much time refactoring and debugging the code as I would have if I’d written it from scratch. This is an important lesson for all of us: while AI can assist, it’s not a substitute for thoughtful design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Practical Code Example: The Good, the Bad, and the Ugly
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let’s say you want to create a simple endpoint to fetch user data in a Flask application. Here’s what Copilot suggested:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Flask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/users/&amp;lt;int:user_id&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Assuming we have a user database
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_user_from_database&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# This part is AI-generated, but what if...
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;__main__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;debug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On the surface, this looks great. However, I quickly realized I needed to handle edge cases—what happens if the user isn’t found? Or if the database connection fails? These are nuances that an AI simply won’t capture, as it operates on patterns rather than the underlying logic of user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Lessons from the Trenches
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most significant lessons I’ve learned is that, while AI can enhance productivity, it can’t take over the critical thinking involved in software development. I often joke that AI is like a really enthusiastic intern: they can help with the grunt work, but you still need to guide them and check their outputs. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I first started integrating AI into my workflows, I felt like I was racing ahead. Yet, I discovered that without a clear vision and architectural framework, I was just spinning my wheels. It’s a bit like cooking: having a fancy new gadget doesn’t make you a chef—you still need skill, creativity, and a good recipe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Embracing the Human Element
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As I reflect on my journey with AI coding agents, I’ve noticed that the most successful implementations are those where human developers actively engage with the tool rather than relying on it blindly. Collaboration is key. When I pair program with an AI, I treat it like a brainstorming partner. I’ll prompt it for suggestions, but I’m always analyzing the outputs and making decisions based on my understanding of the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've also started using AI in a more structured way—incorporating it into specific phases of my development process. For example, during prototyping, I let the AI suggest code snippets, but I still focus on building the architecture and user flow myself. This hybrid approach has saved me time and improved the quality of the final product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Future Thoughts: Navigating the AI Landscape
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Looking ahead, I’m genuinely excited about the potential of AI in development. However, I’ve got some skepticism, too. As these tools evolve, I think we’ll need to address the ethical implications, including issues around code quality, bias in training data, and the impact on jobs in the tech industry. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As developers, it’s essential to remain vigilant and intentional about how we incorporate AI into our workflows. I believe we should view it as a supplement to our skills, rather than a replacement. After all, the craft of coding is an art, and AI shouldn’t rob us of that creative process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Final Takeaways
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, what’s the bottom line? AI coding agents are like a powerful tool in our developer toolkit. They can help generate code, streamline processes, and inspire new ideas, but they won’t build software for us. We need to embrace our human intuition, creativity, and problem-solving skills to create truly great software. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As I continue to navigate this ever-evolving landscape, I encourage you to explore these AI tools, but don’t lose sight of what makes us human developers—our ability to think critically, design thoughtfully, and collaborate effectively. The future of coding may be changing, but our role as creators and innovators remains as crucial as ever. Let’s see where this journey takes us!&lt;/p&gt;




